l expression for it  pause the video and see if you can do  that  all right let's work through it together  well this is once again we're just going  to use our derivative techniques so i  could write y prime of theta the  derivative of y with respect to theta  just going to use the product rule right  over here derivative of this first  expression is 2  cosine of 2 theta cosine of 2 theta  we've already seen that that's just  coming out of the chain rule and then  times the second expression  sine  of theta and then plus  plus the first expression  sine of two theta sine  of two theta  times the derivative of the second  expression derivative of sine of theta  is cosine  of theta fair enough and we do the same  thing for x x prime  of theta  derivative of the first expression it is  going to be  2 times cosine of 2 theta 2 times cosine  of 2 theta times the second expression  cosine  of theta and then you're going to have  the first expression  sine  of 2 theta times the derivative of the  second expression which is negative sine  of theta negative  sine  of theta and we could use this we could  actually evaluate these at points for  example we could say well what's  happening when theta is equal to pi over  4  so when theta is pi over 4 i'll do that  in black right over here  we are going to be at this point  right over there  well let's evaluate it so  if i were to say  y prime  of pi over 4  is equal to let's see  this is going to be equal to  2 cosine of pi over 2 2 times pi over 4  times sine  of pi  over 4  plus  sine of 2 times pi over 4 is sine of  pi  over 2  times cosine of pi over 4  cosine  of pi  over 4. what is this going to be equal  to  well cosine of pi over 2 is 0. so if  that's 0 all of this stuff's going to be  0 and here's sine of pi over 2 this is 1  cosine of pi over 4 is square root of 2  over 2 square root of 2  over 2 so this is going to be equal to  square root of 2 over 2.  and actually just for the sake of saving  some space i'll just w